WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:04.793
Early the next morning Ned and Conseil joined me on the platform.

2
00:00:04.794 --> 00:00:11.215
"Well, Sir Naturalist," said Ned, addressing me in a cheerful tone, "where are we today?"

3
00:00:11.216 --> 00:00:15.469
"We're floating on the surface of the Mediterranean Sea," I replied.

4
00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:20.969
"I don't believe that for one minute," said Ned. "We couldn't have crossed the Isthmus of Suez."

5
00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.969
"If Master says it's true, then it must be true," said loyal Conseil.

6
00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:32.779
"Thank you, Conseil." I described for them how the Nautilus had swiftly passed through Captain Nemo's underwater tunnel.

7
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:41.104
"That shoreline over there is Egypt. You have such good eyes, Ned, that you can probably see the Port Said jetty stretching into the sea."

8
00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:44.017
Ned looked in the direction I pointed.

9
00:00:44.018 --> 00:00:50.866
"Why, I believe you're correct, Professor. We are in the Mediterranean. Good!

10
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:54.929
Now let's discuss our own business where no one will overhear us."

11
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.914
Ned led the way to a small space behind the electric light on top of the submarine.

12
00:00:59.915 --> 00:01:03.596
"What I have to say is very simple," Ned began.

13
00:01:03.597 --> 00:01:11.088
"We're in Europe now. Before Captain Nemo's whims drag us back to the Pacific Ocean, I want to leave the Nautilus

14
00:01:11.089 --> 00:01:17.739
." I didn't want to be responsible for restricting the freedom of my companions, but I really had no desire to

15
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:24.977
leave. Thanks to Captain Nemo and his submarine, I was rewriting my book on deep-sea life while living in the very midst of

16
00:01:24.978 --> 00:01:28.913
it. I didn't want to abandon the Nautilus before my research was

17
00:01:28.914 --> 00:01:33.739
finished. "Ned, do you regret the time you've spent underwater?" I

18
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.977
asked. The harpooner paused for a moment before

19
00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:43.136
replying. Then he crossed his arms and said, "I don't regret it, but I want this voyage to

20
00:01:43.137 --> 00:01:48.628
end." "I have to admit I don't know when that will be," I said. "But it will

21
00:01:48.629 --> 00:01:56.795
end." "I agree with Master," said Conseil. "And it's possible that Captain Nemo will let us go after he has crossed every sea on

22
00:01:56.796 --> 00:02:04.533
earth." "I don't think that will happen." I shook my head. "We know too much about the captain and his submarine for him to let us

23
00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:09.279
go. But in six months, perhaps we'll find another opportunity to

24
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.438
escape." "Six months!" exclaimed Ned. "Where on earth will we be in six

25
00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:23.525
months?" "I don't know," I replied. "But whenever we decide to escape, we must succeed on our first

26
00:02:23.526 --> 00:02:29.192
attempt. If we fail, we'll never have another opportunity, and Captain Nemo will never forgive

27
00:02:29.193 --> 00:02:33.017
us." Ned was still determined to escape as soon as

28
00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:39.089
possible. He outlined a plan that involved taking the small boat one night when we were along the European

29
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.748
coastline. "Do you want to know what I think of your plan?" I

30
00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:46.097
asked. "Certainly, Professor Aronnax," said

31
00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:54.256
Ned. "I think we'll never have the chance to escape because Captain Nemo will always be on guard, especially when we're near Europe." I stood

32
00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:56.797
up. "And now not another word on the

33
00:02:56.798 --> 00:03:02.168
subject. The day that you're ready to escape, let us know and we'll follow you. I leave everything up to

34
00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:05.644
you." The next day we reached the Greek

35
00:03:05.645 --> 00:03:12.493
islands. I wanted to spend some time studying the marine life, but the panels in the gallery remained closed until

36
00:03:12.494 --> 00:03:16.128
evening. After supper Captain Nemo joined me in the

37
00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:24.065
gallery. He seemed preoccupied and didn't speak to me, but kept peering through the windows. Suddenly a man appeared in the

38
00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:29.875
water. "Look, Captain!" I cried. "That poor man has been shipwrecked. We must save

39
00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:33.224
him!" The captain didn't answer me, but leaned against the

40
00:03:33.225 --> 00:03:38.470
window. To my astonishment, the man in the water approached and pressed his face to the glass.

41
00:03:38.471 --> 00:03:42.636
Captain Nemo gave him a signal and the man headed for the surface.

42
00:03:42.637 --> 00:03:45.882
"Don't worry about him," said Captain Nemo.

43
00:03:45.883 --> 00:03:52.961
"That's Nicholas, a bold diver who swims from one Greek island to the next. His nickname is 'The Fish.'"

44
00:03:52.962 --> 00:03:56.866
Captain Nemo went over to a chest in a corner of the room.

45
00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:03.104
I could barely contain my shock as he opened it, revealing a supply of gold bars!

46
00:04:03.105 --> 00:04:10.692
He seemed to have forgotten I was there as he carefully counted the bars. There must have been two tons of gold in that chest.

47
00:04:10.693 --> 00:04:14.867
Where did he get it all and what was he going to do with it? I wondered.

48
00:04:14.868 --> 00:04:18.684
The captain closed the chest and wrote an address on it in Greek.

49
00:04:18.685 --> 00:04:23.859
He pressed a button to communicate with the crew, and within a few minutes four men appeared.

50
00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:28.343
With great difficulty, they removed the heavy chest from the gallery.

51
00:04:28.344 --> 00:04:32.430
Captain Nemo turned to me. "What were you saying, Professor?"

52
00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:37.668
"I wasn't saying anything," I replied, still in shock at what I'd witnessed.

53
00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:39.922
"Then I will bid you good night."

54
00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:43.755
I returned to my cabin very puzzled about what I'd seen.

55
00:04:43.756 --> 00:04:51.747
I tried to fall asleep, but I kept wondering about the connection between the appearance of the diver and the removal of the chest filled with gold.

56
00:04:51.748 --> 00:04:56.986
Soon I felt the rolling movements that signaled the Nautilus had risen to the surface.

57
00:04:56.987 --> 00:05:02.565
I heard steps upon the platform—someone was unfastening the small boat and launching it!

58
00:05:02.566 --> 00:05:06.057
The boat struck the side of the submarine and then all was silent.

59
00:05:06.058 --> 00:05:14.375
Two hours later I heard the same noises and knew that the small boat had returned, and the Nautilus was sinking below the waves.

60
00:05:14.376 --> 00:05:19.883
The next day I told Ned and Conseil about the strange events of the previous evening.

61
00:05:19.884 --> 00:05:24.137
"How much do you think the gold is worth, Master?" asked Conseil.

62
00:05:24.138 --> 00:05:27.716
"Where was the gold taken?" asked Ned.

63
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:33.042
I had no answer for either question, so I returned to working on my notes in the gallery.

64
00:05:33.043 --> 00:05:37.954
Around five o'clock I began to feel so hot that I took off my jacket.

65
00:05:37.955 --> 00:05:44.280
"Perhaps I'm getting a fever?" I thought. I continued my work, but I kept feeling hotter and hotter.

66
00:05:44.281 --> 00:05:47.717
"Could there be a fire onboard?" I asked myself.

67
00:05:47.718 --> 00:05:53.788
I was about to leave the gallery to check when Captain Nemo entered. He went over to the thermometer.

68
00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:56.931
"Forty-two degrees," he said.

69
00:05:56.932 --> 00:06:01.026
"If it gets much hotter, we won't be able to stand it," I said.

70
00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:07.502
"Oh, it won't get any hotter if we don't want it to. We can simply move away from the source that's producing this heat."

71
00:06:07.503 --> 00:06:10.915
"So it's outside?" I was still puzzled.

72
00:06:10.916 --> 00:06:15.669
"Yes, we're floating in a current of boiling water. Look!"

73
00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:20.002
He opened the panels to reveal a white and boiling sea.

74
00:06:20.003 --> 00:06:24.835
"I wanted you to see the unusual spectacle of an underwater volcano."

75
00:06:24.836 --> 00:06:29.581
I pressed my hand to a window, but the heat was so great that I sprang back.

76
00:06:29.582 --> 00:06:34.240
The Nautilus had stopped moving and the temperature in the room was unbearable.

77
00:06:34.241 --> 00:06:38.756
"We can't stay one moment longer in this boiling water!" I cried.

78
00:06:38.757 --> 00:06:42.637
"You're right," said Captain Nemo, as calm as ever.

79
00:06:42.638 --> 00:06:45.994
He gave an order and the submarine turned around.

80
00:06:45.995 --> 00:06:49.716
Fifteen minutes later we were breathing fresh air on the surface.

81
00:06:49.717 --> 00:06:59.119
That's when a startling thought occurred to me: if Ned had chosen this part of the Mediterranean for our escape, we never would have survived.

